From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 06:45:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(db): side stores with their own schema and migration
 lineage

A feature that is not in the main Alembic chain, such as one carried as
a patch on top of Omnigent, had nowhere to keep tables: a revision of its
own in that chain gives every later main revision a second head, and a
database upgraded with it can no longer be upgraded by a build without it.

omnigent/db/side_schemas.py lets such a feature register a side store: a
name, its own metadata and a directory of revisions only it owns. Its
tables are named <name>_<table> and, on PostgreSQL and CockroachDB, live
in the schema omx_<name>; its revision is recorded in its own
<name>_alembic_version. The first use after the main bootstrap creates
the schema and upgrades the store to its head, serialised across servers
by an advisory lock on PostgreSQL and the write lock on SQLite, retried
on CockroachDB and MySQL. The store refuses tables that break the
database guide (foreign keys, auto-increment, JSON, enums, unbounded
columns) or could collide with main names.

Deleting a conversation now tells each registered store which
conversations went, in batches, after the main delete has committed; a
store whose cleanup fails is logged and skipped. The main bootstrap,
CockroachDB's table check and `omnigent debug db-upgrade` see nothing of
a side store, so a build without one starts against a database that has
one. Usage: docs/extending/side_schemas.md.

Not proposed upstream yet: upstream adds tables through its own chain.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
